Explain the context and purpose behind the Lewis and Clark Memorial Column, including its location and any notable features or inscriptions.
The Lewis and Clark Memorial Column is an outdoor monument located at Washington Park in Portland, Oregon. It was created by artist Otto Schumann and dedicated to Meriwether Lewis and William Clark for their historic expedition. The column is made of Snake River granite and stands approximately 34 feet 6 inches tall, with a diameter of 2.5 feet. It features a Classical column design with a sphere on top and sits on a square base that displays bronze seals for the states of Oregon, Washington, Idaho, and Montana, which were part of the Oregon Territory. The monument was commissioned around 1902 by the Lewis & Clark Exposition Commission as a gift from the people of Oregon to commemorate the duo's achievements. President Theodore Roosevelt laid the first cornerstone in 1903, and the column was completed and dedicated in 1908. The monument is administered by the City of Portland's Metropolitan Arts Commission and has been well-maintained, as surveyed by the Smithsonian's "Save Outdoor Sculpture!" program in 1993.
